i, Maharashtra","role":"R&D laboratory; specialised R&D centre for synthesis of specialty and generic aroma chemicals"}],"pre":0,"products":"{\"Flavours & Fragrances\": [\"Customised fragrance compounds\", \"Customised flavour compounds\"], \"Specialty Aroma Ingredients\": [\"Portfolio of over 100 aroma ingredients manufactured using 26 complex chemistries\", \"Astromusk\", \"Evermoss\"], \"Camphor & Terpene Chemicals\": [\"Saraswati (retail camphor brand)\", \"3 Pine (retail camphor brand)\", \"Synthetic camphor\", \"Pharmaceutical-grade camphor\", \"Terpene chemicals\"]}","promises":[],"promises_total":0,"promoters":[{"name":"Mr. Dharmil A. Bodani","role":"Promoter / Executive Director - Chairman and Managing Director (DIN: 00618333); brother of Mr. Shyamal A. Bodani"}],"related":"[{\"name\": \"PT Oriental Aromatics (Indonesia)\", \"relation\": \"subsidiary\", \"note\": \"Overseas subsidiary, 99.86% held by the listed entity; acquired 07th March, 2013; reporting period January to December; disclosed in the related-party note as proposed to be liquidated; does not participate in the …","risks":[{"note":"Global aroma ingredient markets remained buyer-led, limiting pricing power and keeping end-product realisations subdued; in a buyers' market, customer resistance to price increases can delay full recovery of raw material cost inflation.","risk":"Buyer's market and limited pricing power in aroma ingredients"},{"note":"Capacities built by Chinese players continued to flow into non-tariff markets including India, Southeast Asia and parts of Europe, creating intense pricing pressure. OAL responded by prioritising volume leadership, market share and customer retention over short-term margins and by running a CPR (process re-engineering) programme to structurally reduce costs.","risk":"Chinese capacity pressure and dumping in non-tariff markets"},{"note":"The Indian camphor market remained impacted by supply exceeding demand due to substantial domestic capacity additions, and natural camphor imports from China continued to influence pricing; exposure to highly competitive B2B commodity camphor meant low pricing power and margin compression. OAL internalised its heritage brands (Saraswati, 3 Pine, Bhimseni), shifting 60% of the camphor business into the formulated B2C space.","risk":"Domestic camphor overcapacity and product concentration in commodity B2B camphor"},{"note":"Gum turpentine, crude sulphate turpentine and alpha-pinene prices remained elevated while crude-based raw materials were volatile and difficult to source; Rupee depreciation affected the imported portion of the raw material basket across all three divisions. OAL secured feedstock ahead of festive production cycles and held higher inventory when prices were perceived to be lowest.","risk":"Input cost inflation and currency depreciation"},{"note":"Uncertainty over North American tariffs (which briefly reached 50% for certain categories) and global trade instability impacted export volumes and inventory building by U.S. customers; OAL evaluated alternate market channels and, following a new trade deal, is leveraging a restored 20% tariff advantage over Chinese competitors.","risk":"Geopolitical and trade risk / tariff exposure of the export book"},{"note":"Early-stage ramp-up of the Mahad facility, alongside soft aroma ingredient pricing and buyer-market conditions, weighed on profitability; management's priority is to stabilise Mahad and accelerate its commercial ramp-up.","risk":"Execution and margin drag from the Mahad greenfield ramp-up"},{"note":"Rapid asset creation and debt-funded expansion could strain the balance sheet during a soft pricing environment; OAL states it followed a conservative capital structure with net debt-to-equity kept at 0.60x to 0.65x.","risk":"Financial leverage from rapid, debt-funded asset creation"},{"note":"Significant demand fluctuations between quarters (Q1 and Q3 traditionally softer than the festive peak in Q2) can create revenue and cash-flow volatility, while broader macroeconomic uncertainty led to cautious consumer behaviour with fragrances and flavours still perceived at the margin as discretionary. A diversified mix across fragrances, aroma ingredients and camphor is the stated cushion.","risk":"Seasonality and cautious discretionary consumer demand"}],"rpt":{},"rpt_date":"","s":"OAL","sector":"Chemicals","sk":{"as_of":"2026-03-11","cagrs":[{"cagr":"6%-7%","period":"FY 2025-2031","segment":"Indian industrial catalyst market"}],"drivers":"Refinery modernization|Natural gas infrastructure growth|Specialty chemical manufacturing|Structural shift toward cleaner and technology-intensive industrial processes|Recurring replacement cycles and large-scale maintenance programs|Import substitution push under Atmanirbhar Bharat|Increasing …","industry":"Chemicals & Petrochemicals","market_size":null,"overview":"India's industrial catalyst and adsorbent ecosystem plays a critical role in enabling efficient, sustainable, and high-quality production across refining, petrochemical, chemical, fertilizer, and gas-processing industries. These materials, ranging from process-specific catalysts to high-performance adsorbents and inert ceramic support media, are essential for purification, dehydration, sulphur removal, hydro processing, cracking, and environmental compliance. The Indian industrial catalyst industry supports strategically important value chains including Oil & Gas, Petrochemicals, Steel, …","peer_note":"drhp_issuer_selected — issue-driven, not exhaustive","peers":[{"cmp":"₹5,966.65 (closing price on November 28, 2025)","eps":"₹53.33 basic / ₹53.33 diluted","fy":"Fiscal 2025","name":"Linde India Limited","nav":"₹447.91 per share as on 31 March 2025 (calculated as per Annual Report for Fiscal 2025)","pe":"111.88 (as on November 28, 2025)","peer_source":"drhp_issuer_selected","ronw":"11.91% (printed as ROE %)","symbol":null,"total_income":"₹24,853.76 million (revenue for Fiscal 2025; = ₹248,53